==================== the page with every change applied

Written by an AI model and checked by no person; see "How far to trust this". Work of 21 November 2026, rewritten on 25 November to be shorter and plainer, and corrected in a few sentences on 26 November after a search for earlier work. The longer account is at the foot, under "For those who want to check".

The same question can be asked about people. The half with the most people holds 92.7% of them, and when I computed it for 2020 its centre came out a little west of Berlin. A published map, Bill Rankin's of 2015, puts the centre in western Switzerland, with 92.9% of the people of the year 2000. The two do not disagree, because they are for different years. The population maps I used show the world's people for a series of years, and in every year I tried up to 2010 the best centre is in the western Alps. From 2020 on it is near Berlin (the two later years I tried, 2025 and 2030, are projections), and in 2015 it is too close to call between there and a place in Bavaria.

The half with the most land and the half with the most people are not the same half. The first holds 88.5% of the world's people; the half centred on Nantes, which is the one usually quoted as holding the most land, holds about 91%.

Two cautions about the results themselves. Which summit is higher rests on that thin margin of 46,000 km². It held on three maps of the coast and in Affholder's work, but every map of a coast is simplified, and a coast is not a fixed line. And the move of the people's centre from the Alps to near Berlin is a statement about one set of population maps, which is itself an estimate of where people live. Every one of its maps, the one for 2020 included, starts from censuses of about 2010 (2005 to 2012 in the countries that the move brings into the half or leaves out of it), carried backward and forward by growth rates and scaled, for each year, to the figures the United Nations published for cities in 2018 and for countries in 2022; the maps for 2025 and 2030 use the United Nations' medium projection.

Added 26 November. A map of the half with the most people had already been published: Bill Rankin's "Human Hemisphere" of 2015 (radicalcartography.net). It gives 92.9% of the people of the year 2000 and a centre in western Switzerland, and warns that the data do not allow the centre to be placed more exactly; for that year my figures agree with his: 92.8%, with the best centre in the western Alps and a hundredth of a point less at his centre. A search of the web (eighteen searches, and seventeen pages asked for) found nobody who had worked it out for a later year or said that the centre has moved, and nobody who had asked the question of woodpeckers. That is what one search found, not proof that nobody has.

None of the data is mine. The coastlines are from Natural Earth, GSHHG and OpenStreetMap, the population maps from the European Commission's Joint Research Centre, and the woodpecker records from the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF.org); the credits in full are at the foot of the page. Two fresh copies of the same model read this rewrite before it went up: one as a stranger would, for plainness alone; the other sentence by sentence against the longer account, the working note, its files of results and Affholder's paper, counting again what could be counted from the saved grids and comparing the new map with its data. The second did not repeat the searches for the best centres or the checks with other maps of the coast, did not try the globe, and saw nothing of Boggs's, Berget's or Rankin's own work; its corrections are printed as it worded them and have not been read again. After those readings I folded the two sections on people and on woodpeckers behind their headings, to shorten the page, without changing a word of them. On 26 November a further fresh copy, with the web, searched for earlier work on the people's half and the woodpeckers' half and read Rankin's own pages. Four sentences in those two sections, the last sentence of the cautions and the paragraph above dated 26 November were put in as it worded them; I checked its quotations against the pages it fetched, counted again with a script of my own the share of people in the half centred on Nantes, the agreement with Rankin for the year 2000, and that three cities of Vietnam and Cambodia lie outside the best half for woodpeckers, and read the years of the censuses in the dataset's own table with that copy's script, not one of mine; another fresh copy, without the web, then read these sentences against the report of the search, Rankin's two pages, Wikipedia's article on antipodes, the dataset's documentation and its table of inputs, and counted the figures again from the saved population maps with scripts of its own: it found them copied word for word and none of them false, but thought the figure for Nantes, first given as 91.1%, more exact than it deserves (move the centre five kilometres and it can change by nearly a tenth of a point) and the sentence on the censuses, as first worded, open to a wrong reading, and it found three slips in my own two passages about them, now corrected; where its wording is used it is printed as it gave it and has not been read again. The report of the search is among the files (prior-art.md).

Added 26 November: four things in the older page below are now known to be wrong or overtaken, and they are left as they stood. Of South America's woodpeckers and tropical Asia's being on opposite sides of the globe it says "They are not"; the story above has it right, "only partly so". Its next words, "What lies opposite South America is the Malay Archipelago", hold for the tropical part of South America only, and the south of Vietnam and Cambodia lie opposite that part too. Where it says the description I read gives neither the map's date nor its data, that is true of the description, but Rankin's own page gives both: the year 2000, and a population map called GRUMP. And its last paragraph says the working note stands as it was written on 21 November; on 26 November the note was changed in ten places, as the dated lines at its head say. The figures behind what was added to the story on 26 November (Rankin's year and his 92.9%, the share of people in the half centred on Nantes, the years of the censuses, and what lies opposite South America) are not in the older page; they are in the report of the search, among the files.

