==================== the note's head note, from 'What I did with it', with every change applied
by its own documentation every grid, 2020 included, starts from censuses of 2005 to 2012 [those are the years for the countries on the two slivers; over the whole table of inputs they run from 2001 to 2015, with four fifths of the census population in 2009 to 2011, by a later reader's count] scaled to the United Nations' country totals of 2022 [that is, each epoch to the country series the UN published in 2022, and cities to its city series of 2018].
**What I did with it.** Ten changes the reader worded are pasted into the note below (the Rankin sentences, "each of the four grids I ran" for "every grid" twice, the sentence on what lies opposite South America, "published" for "textbook", the island's own count, Arthur's circle, the censuses behind the grids, the sources); the note as it stood is in the public history and in `prior-art-tools/note-before-26nov.txt`. I checked the reader's quotations from Rankin's two pages and from the article on antipodes against the pages it fetched, and recomputed with a few lines of my own (`prior-art-tools/own_check.py`): 92.84% for the half centred on his point in the grid for 2000, 91.05% for the half centred on Nantes in 2020, 771 km from his point to my centre, and the seven places from Guangzhou to Ho Chi Minh City that lie outside the best woodpecker half. Not checked by me: Boggs, Arthur's table, the reader's breakdown of the two slivers by region, and the dataset's documentation beyond the lines the reader quotes and the "Census year" column of its table of inputs, which I read after writing this, with the reader's script (`prior-art-tools/xlsx_dump.py`) and not one of my own, for ten countries on the slivers: the Philippines, Malaysia, Singapore, Indonesia, Thailand, Brazil and Ecuador 2010, Bolivia 2012, Peru 2007, Colombia 2005, as the reader has them. A further fresh instance, without the web, has since read the ten replacements: they are as the reader worded them, and nothing else in the body is changed. It read them against Rankin's two pages and one of his maps, the article on antipodes, the island's page, the text of Arthur's preprint (his table 1 has 28.375, 100.625 and 3386 km; the journal reference it could not check), section 2.5 of the documentation and the table of inputs (Mexico's census year, which the reader could not read, is 2010), and recounted on the grids with a script of its own: 92.837% at Rankin's point and 92.847% at the best in the grid for 2000; 91.05% and 91.07% for two readings of Nantes in 2020, and 90.98% to 91.15% for centres 4 to 5 km away, because the edge of that half passes through Greater Tokyo. It found none of the ten false.*
